  %DocsSection{ title: "Asset Identity" }
    :markdown
      Klenod assets have two stable identifiers:

      - `logical_name`: the source-root-relative file path without import query parameters, such as `images/hero.png`.
      - `output_path`: the canonical root-relative asset path, such as `/hero.640w.abcd1234.png`.
      - `url`: the build-time browser URL, such as `https://cdn.example.test/hero.640w.abcd1234.png`.

      Query parameters configure an import without changing the logical name, so overlapping generated variants can be reused. Asset content is content-hashed, so production servers can cache emitted browser paths aggressively.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Asset Base URLs" }
    :markdown
      Set Klenod's `base` build option to choose the browser-visible asset prefix. It accepts an origin-relative path or an HTTP(S) CDN URL, and normalizes a missing trailing slash:

      ```ruby
      base "/.assets"
      # or: base "https://cdn.example.test"
      ```

      The example web app reads `ASSET_BASE`, defaulting to `/assets/`. Emitted files live directly in `dist/public`; configure the CDN origin or upload mapping to serve that directory at the chosen base. Every emitted asset has a build-time `url` for rendering directly. Generated CSS, JavaScript, image, SVG, and font URLs already use the configured base.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Lookup APIs" }
    :markdown
      The build graph and runtime bundle expose the same lookup shape.

      ```ruby
      context.asset("/home.abc123.css")
      context.assets_for("styles/home.css")
      context.assets_for_module("entrypoint.rb", type: :css)

      bundle.asset("/home.abc123.css")
      bundle.assets_for_module("entrypoint.rb", type: :css)
      ```

      `assets_for_module` is recursive by default. Pass `recursive: false` for only assets directly emitted by the root module. `asset_references_for_module` also includes a graph traversal index, which is useful for route-scoped CSS ordering.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Serving Assets With Rack" }
    :markdown
      Add `klenod-rack` when a Rack application serves assets emitted by a production bundle. `Klenod::Rack::AssetApp` looks up browser paths in the loaded bundle and reads their bytes from the emitted assets directory:

      ```ruby
      # config.ru
      require "klenod/runtime"
      require "klenod/rack"

      bundle = Klenod::Runtime.load_bundle("dist/klenod.bundle")
      entrypoint = bundle.exports("entrypoint")
      application = ->(env) { entrypoint.call(env, bundle) }

      run Klenod::Rack::AssetApp.new(
        bundle,
        assets_dir: "dist/public",
        base: bundle.base,
        app: application
      )
      ```

      The asset app maps public paths under `/assets/` to root-relative files in `assets_dir`, with immutable cache headers, and delegates every other request to `app`, which calls the bundle entrypoint. That entrypoint should accept a Rack environment and return a Rack response. When matching `.br` files are present in `assets_dir`, the asset app also serves Brotli responses to clients that accept them. Pass `base:` to use an origin-relative public prefix such as `/.assets/`; use `path_prefix:` when an external CDN base needs a local development-serving prefix.

  %DocsSection{ title: "CSS, SVG, And Images" }
    :markdown
      CSS imports from Ruby or Haml return class-name maps. CSS imports from CSS become stylesheet dependencies, and CSS `url(...)` references become asset dependencies.

      SVG imports emit the SVG file as a browser asset and export an immutable `SvgMetadata` value with `src`, `width`, `height`, `content_type`, and `aspect_ratio`.

      Raster image imports support `.avif`, `.gif`, `.jpeg`, `.jpg`, `.png`, and `.webp`. Import query options can request widths, formats, and quality:

      ```ruby
      Hero = import("hero.jpg?width=320,640&format=webp,jpeg&quality=82")
      ```

      Ruby image imports return an immutable `ImageMetadata` value with `src`, dimensions, `content_type`, `aspect_ratio`, `variants`, `srcset`, and `sizes`. Its immutable `ImageVariant` values expose the same base metadata plus `format`, `descriptor`, and `quality`. If `format` or `quality` is requested, the default `src` can also be a generated asset. Generated variants are deduplicated by source path, source hash, width, format, and quality.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Google Fonts" }
    :markdown
      `GoogleFontsPlugin` resolves Google Fonts CSS imports, downloads or reuses cached raw CSS, rewrites `fonts.gstatic.com` URLs to local emitted font assets, and emits the rewritten stylesheet as a normal CSS asset. It also emits Capsize-powered metric-adjusted fallback faces by default. Font files are lazy IO-generated assets so downloads can overlap with CPU-bound image generation without becoming unbounded.

